<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tamar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tamar flows about 98 kilometres south from near the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plymouth-sound">Plymouth Sound</a>, forming most of the boundary between Cornwall and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> is said to have fixed it as the border of the Cornish around 936, and it has long been seen as the line between Cornwall and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Brunel's Royal Albert Bridge crosses its estuary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tamar.jpg" alt="River Tamar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Cornfoot · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Tamar,_near_Bohetherick_-_geograph.org.uk_-_977730.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
